---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifeKitchenConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifeKitchenConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="cups-to-grams">
  <UnitsLifeKitchenConverter client:load mode="cups-to-grams" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Turning an American recipe into weights</h2>
    <p>
      Most people need this direction: a recipe in cups, a scale on the counter, and a weight to put on
      it. Pick the ingredient, type the cups (fractions work), and read the grams.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a cup-based recipe, convert it once, write the gram figures in the margin, and do not convert
      again. Each conversion through a rounded cup measure loses a little accuracy; a written weight
      list loses none.
    </p>

    <h2>The staples, in one table</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Ingredient (1 US cup)</th><th scope="col">Grams</th><th scope="col">Ounces</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Flour, all-purpose (spooned)</td><td>123</td><td>4.3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Flour, bread</td><td>128</td><td>4.5</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Flour, cake</td><td>113</td><td>4.0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sugar, granulated</td><td>197</td><td>7.0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sugar, brown (packed)</td><td>210</td><td>7.4</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sugar, icing (unsifted)</td><td>118</td><td>4.2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Butter</td><td>224</td><td>7.9</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Milk, whole</td><td>240</td><td>8.5</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Water</td><td>237</td><td>8.4</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cocoa powder</td><td>84</td><td>3.0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rolled oats</td><td>89</td><td>3.1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rice, uncooked white</td><td>182</td><td>6.4</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      These figures are for the US customary cup of 236.6 mL. If you change the cup standard in the
      tool, all of them change: an Australian metric cup is 5.7% larger, so a cup of flour is 130 g, not
      123 g.
    </p>

    <h2>Fractions are the normal case</h2>
    <p>
      Recipes use halves, thirds and quarters, so the input accepts them directly: <code>1 1/2</code>,
      <code>3/4</code>, <code>2/3</code>, <code>1&frac14;</code> and plain decimals all work. Two thirds
      of a cup of sugar is 131 g, which is much easier to measure accurately than two thirds of a cup.
    </p>

    <h2>Packed, sifted, spooned</h2>
    <p>
      Three words in a recipe change the answer substantially:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Packed: brown sugar pressed into the cup is 213 g, against 145 g loose, a 47% difference.</li>
      <li>Sifted: icing sugar or cake flour aerated before measuring is about 17% lighter than unsifted.</li>
      <li>Spooned and levelled: flour put in gently and levelled is 125 g, against 145 g scooped.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Also check if the recipe says "1 cup flour, sifted" or "1 cup sifted flour". The first means
      measure, then sift; the second means sift, then measure, and gives noticeably less flour.
    </p>

    <h2>Liquids are the easy case</h2>
    <p>
      For water, milk, stock and juice, one millilitre weighs about one gram, so a cup is
      237&ndash;245 g, depending on what is dissolved in it. If you convert only liquids, the volume
      answer is fine and the scale is optional. For dry ingredients, weighing changes the result.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
